<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-25.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="002422"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002422" label="[00:24:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's amazing if both pandoc/haskell and ghostscript can be salvaged</span></div><div class="line" id="002642"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002642" label="[00:26:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">TeX is yet another concern, i'll just keep that in mind i didn't succeed yet when verifying any implementation with the no-c++/tinycc/linux24 profile</span></div><div class="line" id="002811"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#002811" label="[00:28:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not an urgent blocker, because tinycc can bootstrap GNU/c++/, and with it any TeX implementation desired</span></div><div class="line" id="013057"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013057" label="[01:30:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pandoc is not really essential for modern distro</span></div><div class="line" id="014334"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014334" label="[01:43:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is GCC 4.9 basically required to build hugs?</span></div><div class="line" id="014618"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014618" label="[01:46:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh there are patches to build it with newer GCC...</span></div><div class="line" id="014742"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014742" label="[01:47:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, though it's not easy to build it outside Guix...</span></div><div class="line" id="020032"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#020032" label="[02:00:32]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i tried for maybe half an hr yesterday to build GHC 4.09.1, appears it is non-trivially uncompilable with modern GCC too</span></div><div class="line" id="020042"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#020042" label="[02:00:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if i'm reading the Guix declaration correctly, they use GCC 2.95???</span></div><div class="line" id="020056"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#020056" label="[02:00:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i got it to go a bit of a ways with GCC 4.0.4 from live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="020107"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#020107" label="[02:01:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(building using hc-boot)</span></div><div class="line" id="020738"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#020738" label="[02:07:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I don't think I'll be able to easily create gentoo overlay like I had for mrustc/rustc...</span></div><div class="line" id="020751"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#020751" label="[02:07:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(mrustc is now in Gentoo by the way)</span></div><div class="line" id="022824"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022824" label="[02:28:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: well, 4.0.4 can't build unpatched hugs98...</span></div><div class="line" id="022920"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#022920" label="[02:29:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.debian.net/1346694/">https://paste.debian.net/1346694/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="023256"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023256" label="[02:32:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is strange though</span></div><div class="line" id="023305"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023305" label="[02:33:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">crt1.c:(.text._start_c+0x17): undefined reference to `main' ...</span></div><div class="line" id="023327"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023327" label="[02:33:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that doesn't sound like something caused by GCC version</span></div><div class="line" id="023357"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#023357" label="[02:33:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, it's dynamic linking...</span></div><div class="line" id="183906"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#183906" label="[18:39:06]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator rekado we really have ally <a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75745#6">https://issues.guix.gnu.org/75745#6</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="184032"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184032" label="[18:40:32]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: and if anyone knows how to bootstrap Haskell, it's him, considering that *he was the one who did it* in the first place</span></div><div class="line" id="184305"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#184305" label="[18:43:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator considering microhs is in active development and every month (if not every week) there is new feature, it won't be surprising if old ghc versions can be dropped</span></div><div class="line" id="184337"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#184337" label="[18:43:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least that way it won't be painful to bootstrap on riscv (when ghc finally supports it) and arm</span></div><div class="line" id="184714"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184714" label="[18:47:14]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Lennart wrote the original LazyML environment and the HBC compiler, which was then used to bootstrap GHC</span></div><div class="line" id="184854"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184854" label="[18:48:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">unfortunately that bootstrap chain is unusable for us today because 1. LazyML's bootstrap is itself lost (0.99 is the earliest surviving version, and it already requires LazyML 0.95 or newer to compile it), 2. all versions of GHC that could be built with HBC are lost, and 3. the entire bootstrap was carried out on a now extinct mainframe</span></div><div class="line" id="184855"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#184855" label="[18:48:55]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">architecture, as far as what I gathered</span></div><div class="line" id="185050"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185050" label="[18:50:50]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">honestly I am surprised by amount of cooperation, also if ghc is really written conservatively, it's possible to patch relevant source files of ghc that microhs doesn't understand</span></div><div class="line" id="185216"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185216" label="[18:52:16]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">BTW, GHC 9.12 supports riscv, although no official binary packages are supplied yet</span></div><div class="line" id="185342"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185342" label="[18:53:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">time to update this page I guess <a rel="nofollow" href="https://bootstrappable.org/projects.html">https://bootstrappable.org/projects.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="185419"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185419" label="[18:54:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrappable.org last got a real update in 2021...</span></div><div class="line" id="185528"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185528" label="[18:55:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it doesn't mention live-bootstrap, and it hasn't even updated for Guix's full source bootstrap achievement</span></div><div class="line" id="185718"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#185718" label="[18:57:18]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">btw, what about nhc98 is guix? should it be dropped entirely or should it be properly bootstrapped?</span></div><div class="line" id="185932"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185932" label="[18:59:32]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't know - AFAIK it was only included for bootstrapping purposes, which were never realized</span></div><div class="line" id="185943"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185943" label="[18:59:43]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also on bootstrappable.org: &quot;Maintaining GCC version 4.7&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="185954"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#185954" label="[18:59:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">IIRC, most of the work is actually in 4.6</span></div><div class="line" id="190015"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#190015" label="[19:00:15]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">4.7 was the last version without C++ code in it, but 4.6 was the last modular release</span></div><div class="line" id="190704"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#190704" label="[19:07:04]">&lt;ludocode&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: Where is the GCC 4.6/4.7 work going on? Is there a public repository for it?</span></div><div class="line" id="191908"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#191908" label="[19:19:08]">&lt;GoogulatorMobile&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ludocode: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ekaitz-zarraga/gcc">https://github.com/ekaitz-zarraga/gcc</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="192258"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192258" label="[19:22:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yaeh, that's riscv port...</span></div><div class="line" id="192300"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192300" label="[19:23:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, modular release only makes tarball smaller</span></div><div class="line" id="192338"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192338" label="[19:23:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">other than that I dont' think 4.6 vfs 4.7.4 makes much difference anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="192346"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192346" label="[19:23:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">4.7 doesn't unlock any new kernel versions</span></div><div class="line" id="192439"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#192439" label="[19:24:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">GoogulatorMobile: have you tried building hugs outside guix?</span></div><div class="line" id="192633"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#192633" label="[19:26:33]">&lt;GoogulatorMobile&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Not yet</span></div><div class="line" id="192651"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#192651" label="[19:26:51]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas hugs is already package outside of guix in several distros</span></div><div class="line" id="195446"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#195446" label="[19:54:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, Debian and fedora have it...</span></div><div class="line" id="195500"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#195500" label="[19:55:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I can probably check if they have some extra patches to build on modern system</span></div><div class="line" id="201100"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201100" label="[20:11:00]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is nothing fancy about building hugs on modern system, just one patch to inline</span></div><div class="line" id="201638"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#201638" label="[20:16:38]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">btw, would be amazing if <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/blynn/compiler">https://github.com/blynn/compiler</a>  could also bootstrap microhs, then there would be no need to rely on hugs with its nasty bugs and portability problem</span></div><div class="line" id="202351"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202351" label="[20:23:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: hmm, strange, I'm getting some build errors</span></div><div class="line" id="202415"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202415" label="[20:24:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, people here have seen blynn compiler for a while</span></div><div class="line" id="202510"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202510" label="[20:25:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's actually buildable by M2-Planet <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/blynn-compiler">https://github.com/oriansj/blynn-compiler</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="202514"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202514" label="[20:25:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at least some older fork</span></div><div class="line" id="202536"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202536" label="[20:25:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: which patch are you applying?</span></div><div class="line" id="202654"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#202654" label="[20:26:54]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas <a rel="nofollow" href="https://aur.archlinux.org/cgit/aur.git/tree/hsbase_inline.patch?h=hugs">https://aur.archlinux.org/cgit/aur.git/tree/hsbase_inline.patch?h=hugs</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="202901"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#202901" label="[20:29:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, let me generate build log with this patch</span></div><div class="line" id="203004"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#203004" label="[20:30:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: any idea what is going wrong? <a rel="nofollow" href="https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/hugs.txt">https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/hugs.txt</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="203114"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#203114" label="[20:31:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think hugs itself builds but something is wrong with the libraries</span></div><div class="line" id="203222"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#203222" label="[20:32:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it does look to me like it tries to build .so library as a normal binary</span></div><div class="line" id="203241"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#203241" label="[20:32:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but of course it doesn't have main() function, since it is supposed to be a library</span></div><div class="line" id="203330"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#203330" label="[20:33:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if I manually pass -shared to that gcc command then it does build .so file fine</span></div><div class="line" id="204330"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#204330" label="[20:43:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I can workaround this particular issue by adding &quot;-shared&quot; to #define MKDLL_CMD in ./src/config.h but there are some other issues even with this fixed</span></div><div class="line" id="204343"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#204343" label="[20:43:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ERROR &quot;../libraries/bootlib/Foreign/Storable.hs&quot;:149 - Undefined type constructor &quot;HTYPE_INT&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="204942"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#204942" label="[20:49:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas I really have no idea, it seems to be problem caused by autoconf, I am too scared to touch that</span></div><div class="line" id="205005"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205005" label="[20:50:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, looks like it's build system</span></div><div class="line" id="205008"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205008" label="[20:50:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not the compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="205040"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205040" label="[20:50:40]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas to get ../libraries/bootlib/Hugs/Storable.so you need to run: ffihugs ../libraries/bootlib/Hugs/Storable.hs</span></div><div class="line" id="205058"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205058" label="[20:50:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that was missing -shared flag</span></div><div class="line" id="205108"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205108" label="[20:51:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">something wrong in configure I guess</span></div><div class="line" id="205429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205429" label="[20:54:29]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">honestly I don't even want to know why autoconf, automake, configure, cmake, meson, scons, waf were created when it is easy to write portable Makefile or mkfile</span></div><div class="line" id="205504"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205504" label="[20:55:04]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hugs should be forked with all that autohell removed and replaced by plain simple Makefile or mkfile</span></div><div class="line" id="205539"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205539" label="[20:55:39]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I bet there is already mkfile for plan9 as hugs is ported there, just needs adjustment for other platforms</span></div><div class="line" id="205707"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205707" label="[20:57:07]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">people must really hate declarative programming when they don't write Makefile nor mkfile</span></div><div class="line" id="205811"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205811" label="[20:58:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it is posible to write Makefiles, but it's not necesserily easy</span></div><div class="line" id="205821"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205821" label="[20:58:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I've seen people making huge mess out of Makefiles...</span></div><div class="line" id="205840"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205840" label="[20:58:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Makefile is closer to assembly in programming</span></div><div class="line" id="205853"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#205853" label="[20:58:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">whereas cmake or meson are like higher level languages</span></div><div class="line" id="205928"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#205928" label="[20:59:28]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Makefile is not closer to assembly, quite the opposite</span></div><div class="line" id="210002"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210002" label="[21:00:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just state your dependencies and write instructions to create those dependencies, problem solved</span></div><div class="line" id="210032"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210032" label="[21:00:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it scales on multiple processor cores quite naturally</span></div><div class="line" id="210102"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210102" label="[21:01:02]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it mixes declarative and imperative programming</span></div><div class="line" id="210222"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210222" label="[21:02:22]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">entire microhs builds with nothing more than Makefile and is cross-platform</span></div><div class="line" id="210337"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210337" label="[21:03:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, for simple projects yes</span></div><div class="line" id="210348"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210348" label="[21:03:48]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hugs is not rocket science</span></div><div class="line" id="210349"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210349" label="[21:03:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but especially if you look into some big monorepos...</span></div><div class="line" id="210410"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210410" label="[21:04:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">people often make it unreadable</span></div><div class="line" id="210418"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210418" label="[21:04:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, hugs is simple</span></div><div class="line" id="210419"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210419" label="[21:04:19]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">big monorepo - entire plan9 and inferno use mkfile</span></div><div class="line" id="210454"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210454" label="[21:04:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in live-bootstrap we wrote simple makefiles for quite a few GNU and some other tools (perl in particular)</span></div><div class="line" id="210456"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210456" label="[21:04:56]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hell, plan9 easily cross-compiles itself to all machines using mkfile</span></div><div class="line" id="210517"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210517" label="[21:05:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, cross-compilation is more to do with the compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="210520"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210520" label="[21:05:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">than build system</span></div><div class="line" id="210532"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210532" label="[21:05:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, you need to pass some flags or use other binary</span></div><div class="line" id="210610"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210610" label="[21:06:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. M2-Planet binary can create executables for quite a few different platforms...</span></div><div class="line" id="210632"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210632" label="[21:06:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mkfile in plan9 is declared enough to know how to cross-compile to every machine</span></div><div class="line" id="210647"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210647" label="[21:06:47]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and if you read it, it is actually very elegant</span></div><div class="line" id="210658"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210658" label="[21:06:58]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no tricks, everything simple</span></div><div class="line" id="210938"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210938" label="[21:09:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, we e.g. have something like that in <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/steps/patch-2.5.9/mk/main.mk">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/steps/patch-2.5.9/mk/main.mk</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="210957"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#210957" label="[21:09:57]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">build system manages dependencies, you still need it to produce non-conflicting names for all machines at the same time</span></div><div class="line" id="211005"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#211005" label="[21:10:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/tree/master/steps/bash-2.05b/mk">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/tree/master/steps/bash-2.05b/mk</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="211228"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#211228" label="[21:12:28]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">looks similar minus cross-compilation, but considering that is bootstrap it doesn't make sense to cross-compile</span></div><div class="line" id="212318"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#212318" label="[21:23:18]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because if you wanted to cross-compile to arm, riscv, power, mips and so on at the same time, how would you solve race condition like riscv binary replacing arm binary?</span></div><div class="line" id="212605"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#212605" label="[21:26:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in plan9 mk assigns unique names to binaries (because mkfile is declared so) and on &quot;mk install&quot; puts them into /i386/ /arm/ /sparc/ /mips/ and so on (again because mkfile is declared so)</span></div><div class="line" id="212808"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#212808" label="[21:28:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, I didn't try to cross-compile but I did try those makefiles on riscv natively and it worked</span></div><div class="line" id="212809"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#212809" label="[21:28:09]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but this is also for bigger picture, as you can share same disk between multiple machines with different architectures</span></div><div class="line" id="212853"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#212853" label="[21:28:53]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's distributed os that is</span></div><div class="line" id="214705"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#214705" label="[21:47:05]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">forget what I said about hugs on plan9, nobody bothered to write mkfile for it, it uses ape (ansi posix environment) with ugly configure script, even worse not even base package is there, so it can't bootstrap microhs</span></div><br /></div></body></html>